      "why": "iterative is the only family that can plausibly hit 50 MSPS: it has the shortest combinational path (1 micro-rotation/cycle, no k-way or m-way chaining), so its Fmax is highest. With 1 result per N+3 cycles, it needs Fmax >= 50*(N+3) MHz; for N~12-16 that is ~650-850 MHz, which is borderline but the only candidate in my assigned set. I focus data_width 14-20 and n_iter 12-20 to bracket the accuracy constraint (max_abs_err <= 2^-12) while keeping the datapath narrow enough to preserve Fmax and keep power low. angle_guard/frac_guard kept small to limit area/power; both rounding modes explored since rounding can recover ~1 bit of accuracy for free."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "I am restricted to iterative and unrolled_k. The spec demands throughput_msps >= 50, which is the binding constraint. unrolled_k chains k micro-rotations per cycle, lengthening the critical path and lowering Fmax; with 1 result per ceil(N/k)+3 cycles it would need an even higher Fmax than iterative for the same N, so it is far less likely to reach 50 MSPS. I therefore allocate essentially all budget to iterative, where the single-rotation datapath gives the best Fmax and the lowest power_index (the objective). I keep a tiny slice of the search wide enough (data_width 14-20, n_iter 12-20, both rounding modes, small guards) so the Pareto front over power vs accuracy_bits is mapped across the feasible region rather than pinned to one corner. If iterative cannot reach 50 MSPS at any accuracy-feasible point, the correct outcome is to report infeasible rather than relax the constraint. I deliberately do not spend budget on unrolled_k because its longer path makes the throughput constraint harder, not easier, and it would only add power for no throughput benefit in this regime."
}

{
  "decision": "add_family",
  "rationale": "The iterative family is fundamentally incapable of meeting the throughput constraint. It produces 1 result every N+3 cycles. Even with the minimum N=4 (7 cycles/result) and a hypothetical Fmax of 100 MHz, the maximum throughput is ~14.3 MSPS. The best observed throughput was 9.83 MSPS, which is far below the 50 MSPS requirement. To achieve 50 MSPS, we need a pipeline architecture that outputs 1 result per cycle. The 'pipelined' or 'pipelined_m' families are required to meet the throughput constraint. I will add the 'pipelined' family to the search space. Since accuracy is the trade-off objective and we need to minimize power, we should focus on the minimum data_width and n_iter that satisfy the accuracy constraint (max_abs_err <= 2^-12). The iterative results show that ~16-17 iterations and ~17-19 bit widths achieve this. I will set the ranges for the new family to explore this region efficiently.",
